And so those are obviously
linked into Khan Academy India.

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:31.890
There's also a fully Spanish
version of Khan Academy,

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:34.320
es.khanacademy.org that is used

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:36.690
in much of the Spanish speaking world.

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:39.110
I think this is a good push.

00:11:39.110 --> 00:11:40.610
Everything is so fluid, for all I know,

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:42.410
the team might have already done it,

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:44.290
but a lot of the resources
we have in English

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:45.330
we wanna make sure that they're gonna

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:46.770
be available in Spanish as well.

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:49.423
So that will serve a lot of
the Spanish speaking world.

00:11:49.423 --> 00:11:51.330
With the same in Brazilian Portuguese,

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:54.060
we have a lot of usage in Brazil.

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:57.370
We have 40 informal translation
projects around the world

00:11:57.370 --> 00:11:58.600
in other languages.

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:00.910
And so I look forward to coordinating

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:04.270
with all of these, what we
call, our language advocates

00:12:04.270 --> 00:12:07.330
and see how we can get similar resources

00:12:07.330 --> 00:12:08.530
in those geographies.

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:10.460
They're gonna be, in certain languages,

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:12.400
we only have things like math vocalized

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:16.130
in other geographies like in
Spanish speaking Latin America,

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:18.670
we have much more localized.

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:20.453
But that's a really good push, TJ.

00:12:22.090 --> 00:12:23.990
- Yeah, I would add that
our content in Brazil

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:26.020
is also standards aligned, it's aligned

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:28.140
to the BNCC standards.

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:30.310
So we're covered not just in the U.S.

00:12:31.251 --> 00:12:34.320
So Sal, we have a great
question from Yen Bow who asks,

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:36.707
this is gonna be a question
that pops up frequently,

00:12:36.707 --> 00:12:38.800
"How can I not be distracted?

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:40.160
What's the optimal amount of time

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:42.387
to study for a given subject?"

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:47.320
- That's a good question, and
I'm always asking it myself.

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:49.900
You know, there's an
interesting, we had a researcher,

00:12:49.900 --> 00:12:51.940
I believe we published the video

00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:54.080
that came to our office
about a year and a half ago.

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:58.740
And she's pioneered something
called the, which I,

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:01.630
she spreads word about what's
called the Pomodoro Technique.

00:13:01.630 --> 00:13:03.320
It's called that because it's based on a,

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:06.440
it was started by someone using a timer

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:08.460
in the shape of a Pomodoro tomato.

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:11.240
And the Pomodoro Technique is,

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:13.970
is give yourself a fixed amount of time,

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:16.740
let's call it 20 minutes is
what a lot of people talk about,

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:18.260
and you get to set a timer, obviously,

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:19.350
it doesn't have to be a timer

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:21.340
in the shape of a Pomodoro tomato.

00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:23.070
But a timer, you can do it on your phone,

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:25.287
you can do it on a
computer for 20 minutes.

00:13:25.287 --> 00:13:26.850
And say, okay, for this 20 minutes,

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:30.360
I am going to do focused work
on whatever I need to work on.

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:33.460
And then, after you're
done, set the timer again

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:36.010
for 10 or 15 minutes to
give yourself a break.

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:37.580
And actually force the break.

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:39.160
Sometimes after 20 minutes you feel like,

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:42.400
oh, I can keep going, I'm
in the zone, so to speak.

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:43.750
And obviously, if you're
really in the zone,

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:44.730
you don't need to stop,

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:46.220
but by knowing that you have that break,

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:49.180
it actually makes that 20
minutes a lot more focused,

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:50.800
and a lot more productive.

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:53.250
I know sometimes when I
have large blocks of time

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:56.780
and it's unstructured, I kind
of can easily procrastinate,

00:13:56.780 --> 00:13:59.030
find things to delay with.

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:00.227
But then all of a sudden
if I say, oh wait, wait,

00:14:00.227 --> 00:14:03.300
I have a mtg in 30 minutes, let
me finish this right before,

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:04.580
then I can be very focused.

00:14:04.580 --> 00:14:07.310
So that's kind of in line
of the Pomodoro Technique.

00:14:07.310 --> 00:14:10.410
I think on top of that,
especially with things

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:13.320
like school closures
and social distancing,

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:17.580
the more that you can find
a dedicated part of a desk

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:20.530
that you associate with doing
work, you only do work there,

00:14:21.490 --> 00:14:26.490
ideally, if you have enough
space, it's quiet, etc.,

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:29.650
that would be a great addition as well.

00:14:29.650 --> 00:14:32.660
But I think it's, make a list
of the things you need to do,

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:35.130
have a schedule, we've obviously
published some schedules,

00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:38.470
adapt them to your needs,
and then set that timer

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:41.010
and say, okay, this is my
moment, I'm gonna do this.

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:44.620
When you do it, create
a little check list.

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:46.020
It'll get a little dopamine hit

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:49.112
with the neurotransmitter
that makes you happy

00:14:49.112 --> 00:14:51.560
when you get that sense of accomplishment.

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:53.790
Then give yourself that
break, that's important.

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:55.440
Especially in times of social distancing,

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:57.030
try to get a little physical exercise.

00:14:57.030 --> 00:14:59.010
If you can go outside, go outside.

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:01.733
Go for your walk with the
social distance norms.

00:15:02.894 --> 00:15:03.727
And then you come back and you'll be

00:15:03.727 --> 00:15:06.493
that much more energized to
have another 20 minute session.

00:15:08.140 --> 00:15:10.640
- So Sal, we have a Math
and Science teacher,

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:13.820
Samir Mijar and he asks,
"How can Khan Academy

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:16.677
be helpful to me as I teach my students?"

00:15:18.350 --> 00:15:22.370
- Well, that's a big question, Samir.

00:15:22.370 --> 00:15:24.653
If we were talking pre Covid-19,

00:15:25.575 --> 00:15:28.330
and what I'm saying is
still true with Covid-19,

00:15:28.330 --> 00:15:30.013
and hopefully post Covid-19,

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:34.090
our whole focus was to
support teachers like you.

00:15:34.090 --> 00:15:38.220
And every teacher we talk
to talked about the scenario

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:40.540
that they get students
at a certain grade level,

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:42.210
every student has different gaps.

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:43.830
Some of them are ready for the material,

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:46.290
some of them need to
remediate the concepts

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:48.080
from before grade level,
some of them are ready

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:50.310
to move ahead, and if
you're just one teacher

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:53.370
in a classroom of 25, 30, 35 students,

00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:54.730
it's very hard to address

00:15:54.730 --> 00:15:56.570
the particular needs of each of them.

00:15:56.570 --> 00:15:58.120
You know, and at school it's taught

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:00.270
that differentiation is great a practice,

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:02.690
but in practice, it's
actually very hard to do it

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:04.640
when you don't have proper supports.

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:09.150
And so, at Khan Academy,
one of our propositions

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:11.120
or values to teachers are

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:13.780
that we wanna be that
teaching assistant for you.

00:16:13.780 --> 00:16:15.900
We want to help you differentiate.

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:19.500
And so the way that this
has worked in classrooms

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:23.520
is that for 20% of class time at least,

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:26.140
and we see lot of good
advocacy studies around it,

00:16:26.140 --> 00:16:28.790
teachers have students working
at their own time and pace

00:16:28.790 --> 00:16:31.100
on Khan Academy, and as they do so,

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:34.170
teachers get data on
what kids are working on,

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:35.700
what are they mastered, what are they not,

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:37.010
who's engaged, who's not?

00:16:37.010 --> 00:16:39.960
And that is actual data
that teachers can use

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:43.090
to break students out
into more focus groups.

00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:44.470
If they see a bunch of
students having trouble

00:16:44.470 --> 00:16:46.880
in negative numbers, okay,
let me take those five aside,

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:49.380
the other can continue to work.

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:52.240
Then they go back in and continue
working at their own pace.

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:53.220
Let me now work with the kids

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:55.250
who are having trouble with decimals.

00:16:55.250 --> 00:16:58.600
So that is the framework that
we think could work very well

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:00.010
in Math and Science class.

00:17:00.010 --> 00:17:01.550
If you're serving a group of students

00:17:01.550 --> 00:17:02.820
who have significant gaps,

00:17:02.820 --> 00:17:04.390
many of them are behind grade level.

00:17:04.390 --> 00:17:08.260
I've been talking about
Tim Vanderberg a lot,

00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:11.040
who is a amazing teacher
in Hesperia, California.

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:12.320
Ninety percent of his students

00:17:12.320 --> 00:17:14.410
show up in his classroom
below grade level.

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:17.080
He actually does two
things, he simultaneously

00:17:17.080 --> 00:17:18.320
works with the students to learn

00:17:18.320 --> 00:17:20.400
at their own time and pace
at their grade level unit,

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:22.760
and at the same time he
tells all of his students,

00:17:22.760 --> 00:17:24.170
these are sixth grade students,

00:17:24.170 --> 00:17:26.640
to start as early as early-learning

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:29.470
and a third grade math an
arithmetic on Khan Academy

00:17:29.470 --> 00:17:31.900
and do that at their own
time and pace as well

00:17:31.900 --> 00:17:34.460
so that they can fill in
all these Swiss cheese gaps

00:17:34.460 --> 00:17:36.780
that might have accumulated over time.

00:17:36.780 --> 00:17:39.940
In this Covid-19 world,
everything I just described

00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:43.970
is still operable, and those
schedules we've published

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:45.850
talk about how it could work for students

00:17:45.850 --> 00:17:46.890
of different age groups

00:17:46.890 --> 00:17:50.350
as they're now not being
able to work from school.

00:17:50.350 --> 00:17:52.810
And as a teacher, I think,
one of the powers is

00:17:52.810 --> 00:17:55.750
you can continue to monitor
that data from your home,

00:17:55.750 --> 00:17:57.380
see what the students are working on,

00:17:57.380 --> 00:17:58.930
where they're strong, where they're not,

00:17:58.930 --> 00:18:00.070
what they need help in.

00:18:00.070 --> 00:18:03.310
And if you see that, wow,
I still have five or six

00:18:03.310 --> 00:18:05.690
of my kids who are really
struggling with this concept,

00:18:05.690 --> 00:18:08.170
then you could schedule a
video conference with them.

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:11.000
You could say, hey, why doesn't
everyone come on, you know,

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:13.520
if you're using some kind of
a learning management system,

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:16.310
say Google Classroom, you
can see here's a link to Zoom

00:18:16.310 --> 00:18:20.100
for you five, I'm gonna assign
this to you five students,

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:24.160
please join me at 2pm Eastern
time, making things up,

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:26.490
we're gonna do a focus
session on negative numbers

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:27.780
'cause I'm assuming you've already used,

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:29.360
you've looked at the
hints on Khan Academy,

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:31.310
you've looked at the videos and
you're still having trouble,

00:18:31.310 --> 00:18:32.143
I wanna work with you.

00:18:32.143 --> 00:18:34.510
And then you could schedule
another for other kids.

00:18:34.510 --> 00:18:37.750
And then maybe you can have
whole class video conferencing

00:18:37.750 --> 00:18:39.690
to say, okay, we'll see
how everyone's working.

00:18:39.690 --> 00:18:42.240
What can I do, get
feedback from some students

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:43.390
to unblock them more.

00:18:43.390 --> 00:18:45.940
So I think that whole world of things

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:48.290
are things that could be very powerful

00:18:48.290 --> 00:18:50.450
in this now distributive remote world

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:51.923
that we find ourselves in.

00:18:54.020 --> 00:18:55.870
Other question-
- Sal, we have a question.

00:18:55.870 --> 00:18:57.710
We have a question from Jaylon McCann,

00:18:57.710 --> 00:18:59.637
it's actually one that I wanna know too.

00:18:59.637 --> 00:19:02.357
"Do you have all the Black Hole Badges?"

00:19:04.730 --> 00:19:08.343
- It's amazing how much people
are into Black Hole Badges.

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:11.480
So simple answer is, for those of you

00:19:11.480 --> 00:19:13.130
who don't know what a Black Hole Badge is

00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:14.620
or don't even know about badging,

00:19:14.620 --> 00:19:18.750
we have badges on Khan Academy
for various accomplishments.

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:20.940
And the highest badge
is a Black Hole Badge.

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:22.500
And we always wanted to make that

00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:26.100
for really exceptional things.

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:29.340
Now, one of the things that happened,

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:31.670
this was about seven or eight
years ago at Khan Academy,

00:19:31.670 --> 00:19:34.390
I would go and answer people's questions

00:19:34.390 --> 00:19:38.960
when they asked them in
our discussion boards.

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:41.520
And people didn't believe that it was me.

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:46.520
And so we needed some way
to give evidence to people

00:19:46.530 --> 00:19:48.640
that that account was actually my account.

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:52.290
And so what we did was we
gave me a Black Hole Badge

00:19:52.290 --> 00:19:54.440
called a This Is Sal Black Hole Badge.

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:57.320
And I think then the
engineer who did that,

00:19:57.320 --> 00:20:00.190
he just was trying to maybe butter me up

00:20:00.190 --> 00:20:03.450
and he gave me a couple
of more Black Hole Badges.

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:05.400
And so I am one of the few people

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:07.470
who has a Black Hole Badge.

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:10.500
I believe there are others and there's

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:12.440
in different times in
history different ways

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:15.330
to address it, but given
how many questions I get

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:16.990
about Black Hole Badges from students,

00:20:16.990 --> 00:20:19.990
I do think all of us at Khan Academy

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:22.600
have a duty to you to be clear

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:24.700
about what it takes to achieve one

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:26.320
and how you can get there
'cause it seems like

00:20:26.320 --> 00:20:28.583
that is motivating for a lot of folks.

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:31.470
- Great, thanks Sal.

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:34.643
We have a question from another
teacher, Charlene Seuss,

00:20:37.464 --> 00:20:41.113
Our students at Khan Academy
use MAPPERS for Math, love it,

00:20:41.988 --> 00:20:45.810
is there a plan for Khan Academy
to do this with reading?"

00:20:45.810 --> 00:20:47.810
- That's a great question.

00:20:47.810 --> 00:20:50.128
So for those who all, who
don't know what that is

00:20:50.128 --> 00:20:52.380
there's something called
the MAP Growth Assessment

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:55.930
that's taken by 20% of grades
three through 8th students

00:20:55.930 --> 00:20:57.330
in the United States.

00:20:57.330 --> 00:20:59.410
And it measures student growth

00:20:59.410 --> 00:21:00.670
which is a really powerful thing.

00:21:00.670 --> 00:21:02.500
In fact, most of the advocacy studies

00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:05.860
that we use to measure
Khan Academy advocacy,

00:21:05.860 --> 00:21:07.620
we use the MAP Growth Assessment.

00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:10.880
They're run by this
non-profit called the NWEA.

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:13.230
And they really are the
gold standard in growth.

00:21:13.230 --> 00:21:16.500
And so the MAPPER was
actually a very simple,

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:18.590
it was actually a (mumbles) project

00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:21.300
from some of our team
members a couple of years ago

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:23.950
to be able to say, hey,
if a student is scoring

00:21:23.950 --> 00:21:26.670
in this range on the MAP
what might be appropriate

00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:29.070
for them to work on Khan Academy?

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:32.050
Done since then is we've
actually reached out

00:21:32.050 --> 00:21:35.570
to the NWEA and we formed
a partnership with them.

00:21:35.570 --> 00:21:38.780
And what we're rolling out
which is a more (mumbles)

00:21:38.780 --> 00:21:41.460
where it's actually integrated
with the MAP Assessment.

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:43.720
And that's something that
we're doing with districts.

00:21:43.720 --> 00:21:46.660
And so for any of you teachers out there

00:21:46.660 --> 00:21:48.770
what happens is students
take the MAP Assessment

00:21:48.770 --> 00:21:51.530
and it automatically populates

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:53.380
personalized learning
plans on Khan Academy,

00:21:53.380 --> 00:21:55.930
we call it the MAP
Accelerator on Khan Academy.

00:21:55.930 --> 00:21:57.300
Teachers can adjust it.

00:21:57.300 --> 00:22:00.030
We really pride ourselves in our ability

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:01.670
to give teachers (mumbles)

00:22:01.670 --> 00:22:03.310
'cause they know the students best.

00:22:03.310 --> 00:22:05.340
But then students can work
at their own time and pace.

00:22:05.340 --> 00:22:08.430
And then when they take
the next MAP Assessment,

00:22:08.430 --> 00:22:11.130
the growth that's measured,
we can then start to see,

00:22:11.130 --> 00:22:13.510
hopefully, associations between
the time on Khan Academy

00:22:13.510 --> 00:22:14.480
and the growth on the MAP.

00:22:14.480 --> 00:22:16.810
So the MAP acts for
placement on Khan Academy

00:22:16.810 --> 00:22:19.430
for personalization and then
that work on Khan Academy

00:22:19.430 --> 00:22:22.340
we can see how much it
drives student growth.

00:22:22.340 --> 00:22:25.170
And so that is something,
this is a new muscle

00:22:25.170 --> 00:22:26.320
we're building for Khan Academy.

00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:28.250
We're doing it officially
with the districts

00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:31.020
so if that's something
of interest to folks,

00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:35.140
I encourage you to talk
to your school principal,

00:22:35.140 --> 00:22:37.980
the district's
superintendent and say, hey,

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:40.040
we should think about
doing this MAP Accelerator.

00:22:40.040 --> 00:22:42.440
And the reason why it's
a district thing is

00:22:42.440 --> 00:22:43.940
there's technical things like rostering,

00:22:43.940 --> 00:22:46.410
and making sure that
the student IDs all work

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:49.320
throughout the district
so that it syncs well

00:22:49.320 --> 00:22:52.570
with things like the MAP Assessment.

00:22:52.570 --> 00:22:53.720
The question is are we going

00:22:53.720 --> 00:22:55.760
to go into English and Language Arts?

00:22:55.760 --> 00:22:59.530
So the simple answer is, we
don't have plans on MAPPERS

00:22:59.530 --> 00:23:02.770
to do that, but MAP
Accelerator we do have plans

00:23:02.770 --> 00:23:04.960
and I hope that that will be available

00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:09.290
in the next several years
to answer your question.

00:23:09.290 --> 00:23:10.910
Other questions.

00:23:10.910 --> 00:23:12.590
- So Sal we have one more question

00:23:12.590 --> 00:23:14.060
before we have to close out today.

00:23:14.060 --> 00:23:16.280
So this one's from Lucas Muldrop.

00:23:16.280 --> 00:23:17.860
What do you think Khan Academy's

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:19.393
biggest weakness is right now?

00:23:20.810 --> 00:23:22.203
- Our biggest weakness.

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:28.440
So I'm very aware, I could
go through a huge list.

00:23:28.750 --> 00:23:32.090
I think there's a couple,
we definitely have gaps

00:23:32.090 --> 00:23:34.490
in certain subjects and grades.

00:23:34.490 --> 00:23:36.790
And people on this that I've talked about

00:23:36.790 --> 00:23:38.670
we have Khan Academy
Kids which is reading,

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:40.870
writing, social (mumbles)
learning and math.

00:23:42.590 --> 00:23:44.740
The core of math we have from Pre-K

00:23:44.740 --> 00:23:47.070
all the way through
Calculus and Statistics.

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:48.830
English and Language Arts is a new muscle

00:23:48.830 --> 00:23:49.980
that we are building.

00:23:49.980 --> 00:23:52.430
And there's only certain aspects of it

00:23:52.430 --> 00:23:54.170
that we can tackle with the modalities

00:23:54.170 --> 00:23:56.000
that have on Khan Academy.

00:23:56.000 --> 00:23:58.320
We have what's called the beta version

00:23:58.320 --> 00:24:00.990
which is almost a pre-release version

00:24:00.990 --> 00:24:02.540
of our English and Language Arts.

00:24:02.540 --> 00:24:05.140
It has reading comprehension passages,

00:24:05.140 --> 00:24:07.140
students can answer questions,

00:24:07.140 --> 00:24:08.770
but it still doesn't have all

00:24:08.770 --> 00:24:11.220
of the mastery of mechanics yet.

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:12.710
Obviously, we haven't figured out ways

00:24:12.710 --> 00:24:15.960
to give students writing practice per se

00:24:15.960 --> 00:24:18.070
at those grade levels, so that's a gap.

00:24:18.070 --> 00:24:21.740
I think we need to figure
out middle school Science.

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:23.820
We have a gap there.

00:24:23.820 --> 00:24:25.387
I've recommended that a lot
of middle school students

00:24:25.387 --> 00:24:27.660
can work on high school biology right now

00:24:27.660 --> 00:24:28.800
'cause it's very relevant to the world

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:31.050
and I think they have
the background to do it,

00:24:31.050 --> 00:24:35.280
to start learning about
viruses and RNA and DNA.

00:24:35.280 --> 00:24:36.370
And I think in high school

00:24:36.370 --> 00:24:38.510
we actually have many more subjects,

00:24:38.510 --> 00:24:40.770
Math, Science and Humanities.

00:24:40.770 --> 00:24:42.750
I think there's more to add.

00:24:42.750 --> 00:24:47.620
I think another gap that
I think is super important

00:24:47.620 --> 00:24:51.500
is we are building tools that the students

00:24:51.500 --> 00:24:52.770
can learn at their own time and pace.

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:55.770
We can give data to students,
to parents and teachers.

00:24:55.770 --> 00:24:59.350
But we don't view that as a substitute

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:01.640
for the live in-person
interaction as you heard

00:25:01.640 --> 00:25:05.600
from the previous teacher's
question, Samir's question.

00:25:05.600 --> 00:25:07.900
The ideal is that this
is used in conjunction

00:25:07.900 --> 00:25:11.160
with a live teacher or a parent,

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:12.150
or if a student is stuck,

00:25:12.150 --> 00:25:14.370
they can get motivated to keep engaging,

00:25:14.370 --> 00:25:15.620
power through those failures

00:25:15.620 --> 00:25:17.970
which are really powerful
learning experiences.

00:25:19.561 --> 00:25:22.520
Teachers can take 'em
into breakout sessions.

00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:24.570
And we were seeing a
lot of success with that

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:26.750
in physical classrooms during, you know,

00:25:26.750 --> 00:25:28.220
what you could call blended environments

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:30.340
where kids are working on Khan Academy

00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:32.990
and they're able to get
either peer to peer help

00:25:32.990 --> 00:25:35.090
or help from their teachers.

00:25:35.090 --> 00:25:38.060
I think in this time of school shutdowns

00:25:38.060 --> 00:25:41.040
and the Covid-19 situations,

00:25:41.040 --> 00:25:43.940
I think that in-person
interaction is even more important

00:25:43.940 --> 00:25:45.170
because we're all socially distant,

00:25:45.170 --> 00:25:47.450
but obviously we can't be
in the same room together.

00:25:47.450 --> 00:25:49.630
And so that's why in our calendar

00:25:49.630 --> 00:25:51.530
and on this live stream
rather than saying,

00:25:51.530 --> 00:25:54.760
it'd be amazing if teachers
and parents and students

00:25:54.760 --> 00:25:58.250
could create viewer conferencing sessions

00:25:58.250 --> 00:26:00.430
in parallel to the Khan Academy sessions

00:26:00.430 --> 00:26:04.160
to be able to do some of that
live in-person interaction.

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:06.150
So I think that's a
weakness for Khan Academy.

00:26:06.150 --> 00:26:09.450
I think it can be complemented
with other things.

00:26:09.450 --> 00:26:10.720
I think there's many other weakness

00:26:10.720 --> 00:26:12.550
I could get into the
detail of the product.

00:26:12.550 --> 00:26:14.130
Our team here, we have a big list of stuff

00:26:14.130 --> 00:26:16.480
that we hope to tackle
over the next few years.

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:18.080
And actually that's a good segue

00:26:19.060 --> 00:26:21.550
for me to just kind of close out.

00:26:21.550 --> 00:26:23.760
In my closeout, first
of all, thanks everyone

00:26:23.760 --> 00:26:26.840
for joining, as I said, in
all of our live streams.

00:26:26.840 --> 00:26:29.260
I think this is now the 6th, 7th one

00:26:29.260 --> 00:26:30.840
that we've been doing.

00:26:30.840 --> 00:26:32.430
I hope you're finding this useful.

00:26:32.430 --> 00:26:34.640
I actually find this incredibly satisfying

00:26:34.640 --> 00:26:35.910
to stay connected with folks

00:26:35.910 --> 00:26:38.880
in a world where we are socially distanced

00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:41.240
and if this can help
people feel more connected,

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:43.600
that's a huge, huge thing.

00:26:43.600 --> 00:26:46.680
And we are doing everything on our side

00:26:46.680 --> 00:26:48.760
that's possible to be able
to step up to this crisis.

00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:52.913
We're seeing our server cap...
our load had up more than 2X.

00:26:53.875 --> 00:26:55.930
So things like our costs are going up,

00:26:55.930 --> 00:26:57.060
and I just wanna remind everyone

00:26:57.060 --> 00:26:59.100
we are a not for profit organization,

00:26:59.100 --> 00:27:02.410
we are funded through
philanthropic donations.

00:27:02.410 --> 00:27:05.140
Many of the gaps, the
weaknesses we just talked about

00:27:05.140 --> 00:27:07.640
on Khan Academy, these are
things we would love to fill,

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:09.130
but we need resources to do it.

00:27:09.130 --> 00:27:12.580
We need donations from
folks across the board

00:27:12.580 --> 00:27:15.330
to make this a possibility.

00:27:15.330 --> 00:27:17.840
We've gotten incredible support

00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:19.170
from some corporate partners.

00:27:19.170 --> 00:27:21.500
If any of you are parents
working, distribute it,

00:27:21.500 --> 00:27:23.800
try to get your corporations
involved with us.

00:27:23.800 --> 00:27:25.410
Folks, Bank of America was the first

00:27:25.410 --> 00:27:29.150
to step up last weekend
followed closely by AT&amp;T,

00:27:29.150 --> 00:27:32.760
Google.org, and then most recently,

00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:33.593
well I'll announce it tomorrow.

00:27:33.593 --> 00:27:35.680
I'm not sure if it's public yet.

00:27:35.680 --> 00:27:38.290
But that's just kind of
a, that's helping a lot

00:27:38.290 --> 00:27:39.630
and we need more corporations.

00:27:39.630 --> 00:27:43.490
But our costs, our servers
were millions of dollars

00:27:43.490 --> 00:27:46.700
before the crisis and now
they're going to be double that.

00:27:46.700 --> 00:27:49.120
On the order of it was $6,000,000 a year

00:27:49.120 --> 00:27:50.970
on just server costs alone much less

00:27:50.970 --> 00:27:52.270
we have a team of 200 folks

00:27:52.270 --> 00:27:55.760
who are helping develop the
content, writing the software,

00:27:55.760 --> 00:27:57.750
partnering with school
districts, etc. etc.

00:27:57.750 --> 00:28:00.050
So our costs are going up and this crisis

00:28:00.050 --> 00:28:01.310
and we need your help.

00:28:01.310 --> 00:28:03.480
We're running a significant deficit

00:28:03.480 --> 00:28:05.870
that we're not sure how long we can do it.

00:28:05.870 --> 00:28:09.350
So anyway, thanks everyone
for being a part to this.

00:28:09.350 --> 00:28:12.020
I will also close out with stay safe,

00:28:12.020 --> 00:28:13.850
stay socially distanced, I'm gonna work

00:28:13.850 --> 00:28:15.010
on those meditation videos

00:28:15.010 --> 00:28:16.850
'cause I think the most important thing is

00:28:16.850 --> 00:28:18.460
take care of yourself right now and then

00:28:18.460 --> 00:28:20.250
that's going to put you in a
position where you can start

00:28:20.250 --> 00:28:23.140
thinking about how you can take of others.

00:28:23.140 --> 00:28:25.040
Thank you so much, thanks for joining.

